  "description": "Learn The Reticular Formation: Arousal Control Center in one minute.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Deep inside the brainstem lies a tangled web of neurons that decides whether you stay awake or fall asleep.",
      "visual": "🕸️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "The reticular formation is a diffuse network stretching from the medulla to the midbrain.",
      "visual": "🧬"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "It regulates arousal levels by sending signals upward to the cortex, keeping it alert or letting it rest.",
      "visual": "📡"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Researchers discovered its role by accident: cutting through it in animal experiments caused permanent unconsciousness.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "It also filters repetitive stimuli, which is why you stop noticing a ticking clock after a few minutes.",
      "visual": "⏰"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "A scattered net of brainstem neurons holds the switch between waking life and sleep.",
      "visual": "🌙"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What does the reticular formation primarily control?",
    "options": [
      "Arousal and sleep-wake transitions",
      "Visual processing",
      "Language comprehension"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
